<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippines</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Philippines is an archipelago in South-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from the sixteenth century and named after Philip II. It remained one of Spain's last colonies after the American independence wars, until it was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>, leaving lasting legacies of resentment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippines.jpg" alt="Philippines" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Philippines consists of about 7,641 islands with a total area of about 300,000 km2, stretching 1,850 kilometres from north to south. They fall into three divisions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, and the 11 largest islands make up about 95 percent of the land. The highest mountain is Mount Apo on Mindanao, at 2,954 metres.</p><p>The islands lie on the western fringes of the Pacific Ring of Fire, with 23 active volcanoes and about five recorded earthquakes a day, most too weak to be felt. On an expedition of the 16th century Ruy López de Villalobos named Leyte and Samar Felipinas after the Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, later Philip II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and the name Las Islas Filipinas was later used for the Spanish possessions. The title Republic of the Philippines appeared in the 1935 constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Evidence of early hominins dates to as early as 709,000 years ago, and bones from Callao Cave may represent an unknown species, Homo luzonensis, who lived 50,000 to 67,000 years ago. The first Austronesians reached the islands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> around 2200 BCE and settled the Batanes Islands and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>. The earliest known surviving written record is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laguna-copperplate-inscription">Laguna Copperplate Inscription</a> of 900 CE, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a>. By the 14th century large coastal settlements had become trading centres, trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> had begun under the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> and widened under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulu-archipelago">Sulu Archipelago</a> by the 15th century.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a>'s arrival marked the beginning of Spanish colonisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> became the dominant religion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> became the western hub of trans-Pacific trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> began in 1896 and became entwined with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> of 1898, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded the territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and revolutionaries declared the First Philippine Republic. The ensuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> left the United States in control until the Japanese invasion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the Philippines became independent in 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> ruled under martial law from 1972 to 1981, and the People Power Revolution overthrew him in 1986. The Philippines is a constitutional republic with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a>, in which the president serves a six-year term, and its population was 112,729,484 in July 2024. The largest ethnic group in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a>, at 26.0 percent, and indigenous peoples comprise 110 ethnolinguistic groups. Spanish and American rule left lasting marks, among them the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, the use of English and Spanish surnames resulting from an 1849 edict.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philippines” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brunei's power in the <b>Philippines</b> was challenged by Spain, whose forces under Miguel López de Legazpi took Manila from its Muslim rulers, relatives and allies of Brunei, in 1570–1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The kilns of Gò Sành near Vijaya made olive-green and brown glazed pottery, at its peak in the 14th to 16th centuries and found as far away as Egypt, the Emirates, Malaysia and the <b>Philippines</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined the <b>Philippines</b> as one of only two predominantly Catholic states in Asia and is the only sovereign country in Asia with Portuguese as an official language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The destruction wrought across China, Korea, the <b>Philippines</b>, and Southeast Asia left resentments that affected regional geopolitics for decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It spread the Castilian language, Catholicism and Spanish institutions across most of the Americas and to the <b>Philippines</b>, and its silver drove trade from Europe to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…spread to Jambi, Kampar, Bengkalis, Siak, Aru and the Karimun Islands, through the peninsula, to Java and the <b>Philippines</b>; the rulers of Kampar and Indragiri converted through Muzaffar Shah's influence, and two of Java's Wali Sanga, Sunan Bonang and Sunan Kalijaga, are said to have studied in Malacca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
